WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.116
So far you reached hello-devops with a NodePort, or with kubectl port-forward.

2
00:00:08.116 --> 00:00:09.922
Both are fine on a laptop.

3
00:00:09.922 --> 00:00:12.213
Neither is how real users arrive.

4
00:00:12.433 --> 00:00:22.381
For traffic from outside, you usually want one entry point for many apps, routing by host name and path, TLS in one place, and gradual releases.

5
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:25.853
A Service of type LoadBalancer gives you an address.

6
00:00:25.853 --> 00:00:28.480
But it works with connections, at layer 4.

7
00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:31.180
The request carries a path and headers.

8
00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.597
A layer 4 Service never looks at them.

9
00:00:33.597 --> 00:00:35.696
To it, this is only a connection.

10
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.670
Routing on those needs a proxy that understands HTTP, at layer 7.

11
00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:44.852
Plus a Kubernetes object that tells the proxy what to do.

12
00:00:45.067 --> 00:00:47.443
The older API for this is Ingress.

13
00:00:47.443 --> 00:00:49.121
It was small on purpose.

14
00:00:49.121 --> 00:00:50.589
Hosts, paths and TLS.

15
00:00:50.589 --> 00:00:56.251
Everything else went into annotations, which each controller defined its own way.

16
00:00:56.467 --> 00:00:59.153
The Ingress API is now frozen.

17
00:00:59.153 --> 00:01:06.139
And ingress-nginx, the most popular controller, was retired on March 24, 2026.

18
00:01:06.139 --> 00:01:09.095
The newer API is the Gateway API.

19
00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:11.988
It splits the job by who owns what.

20
00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:14.988
A GatewayClass says which controller handles it.

21
00:01:14.988 --> 00:01:17.863
The platform team creates it once per cluster.

22
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:23.492
A Gateway is an entry point, with listeners, such as HTTP on port 80.

23
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:27.362
And an HTTPRoute holds the routing rules for one app.

24
00:01:27.362 --> 00:01:30.817
App teams own these, and attach them to a Gateway.

25
00:01:31.533 --> 00:01:33.349
Now apply the lab's folder.

26
00:01:33.349 --> 00:01:36.645
Envoy Gateway's controller watches these objects.

27
00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:37.537
Step one.

28
00:01:37.537 --> 00:01:40.067
The class eg names the controller.

29
00:01:40.067 --> 00:01:44.161
Envoy Gateway sees its own name, and accepts the class.

30
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:45.044
Step two.

31
00:01:45.044 --> 00:01:47.000
The Gateway uses class eg.

32
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.792
So the controller creates an Envoy proxy, a Deployment and a Service, for it.

33
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:53.829
Step three.

34
00:01:53.829 --> 00:01:59.861
The HTTPRoute attaches to hello-gateway, and points at the hello-devops Service.

35
00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:00.721
Step four.

36
00:02:00.721 --> 00:02:05.959
The controller turns the route into Envoy settings, and sends them to the proxy.

37
00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:07.596
Now it starts forwarding.

38
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:11.493
A request now goes from curl, to Envoy, to hello-devops.

39
00:02:11.493 --> 00:02:13.406
Look where the controller is.

40
00:02:13.406 --> 00:02:15.186
It never handles a request.

41
00:02:15.186 --> 00:02:18.088
It only watches objects and writes settings.

42
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:23.115
On kind there is no cloud, so the proxy's Service never gets a real address.

43
00:02:23.115 --> 00:02:24.635
The routing still works.

44
00:02:24.635 --> 00:02:26.789
You reach Envoy with port-forward.

45
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:28.377
Now a canary.

46
00:02:28.377 --> 00:02:32.697
A second version of the app, hello-canary, gets its own Service.

47
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.361
The lab's canary route has two rules.

48
00:02:35.361 --> 00:02:36.891
Look at rule two first.

49
00:02:36.891 --> 00:02:40.749
It sends traffic to both Services, with weights 90 and 10.

50
00:02:40.967 --> 00:02:45.965
So about nine of every ten requests go to hello-devops, and one to the canary.

51
00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:51.894
Envoy picks a backend at random for each request, in proportion to the weights.

52
00:02:51.894 --> 00:02:55.375
So fifty requests rarely split exactly 45 and 5.

53
00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.194
That is a canary release.

54
00:02:57.194 --> 00:03:02.995
The new version gets a small share of real traffic, while you watch its errors and latency.

55
00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:04.863
Rule one is for testers.

56
00:03:04.863 --> 00:03:07.843
A request with the header x-canary, always.

57
00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:11.770
It also has the path slash, so both rules match it.

58
00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:12.859
Which one wins?

59
00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:14.915
The most specific match wins.

60
00:03:14.915 --> 00:03:18.103
A header match is more specific than a path alone.

61
00:03:18.103 --> 00:03:20.781
So this request always goes to the canary.

62
00:03:20.781 --> 00:03:22.693
You never order rules by hand.

63
00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.413
If the canary looks good, you move the weights.

64
00:03:26.413 --> 00:03:31.541
If not, you set the canary to zero, and every request goes back to hello-devops.

65
00:03:33.767 --> 00:03:36.643
The objects on top only configure the proxy.

66
00:03:36.643 --> 00:03:41.481
The proxy carries the real requests, and splits them between the Services.
